 caused others to grow gas masks on  their face  and they are all asking the same  question  with the condition having gone airborne  the team has rushed to a supposedly  empty alien medical capsule and realized  that escaped microscopic medical  machines called nana jeans  are the cause of all the trouble captain  jack teleports out  the doctor pushes young nancy who's been  claiming to be the child's sister since  the beginning of the story  to answer that repeated question  truthfully nancy has been hiding from  the truth  her entire life the truth that  jamie the boy who became the empty child  isn't  her brother but her son  this is all done well slowly built up  the work by florence hoath  as nancy is spot on capturing the pain  of having to say  the truth that she has been running from  and the shame  of having hidden it from even her  own child and then comes the first  glimpse of what this truly means  for the ninth doctor  come on please this is where the facade  of confidence that the doctor just used  to get nancy to reach out  drops away and we get to see how truly  desperate he is yeah  he had a plan but he didn't know it  would work this  line delivery makes it clear that he is  more than prepared to  plead in the hope that maybe  just maybe this will work  as the microscopic nanogenes recognize  the parent dna  they come to realize how they have been  misunderstanding what humans are  supposed to be  even as the whole thing looks like it's  worked  the doctor still can't quite bring  himself to have full confidence in that  and he pleads one more time  oh come on give me a day like this  give me this one this line from  eccleston  isn't about winning this day it's about  every day  where he didn't win every day where  people  died and he couldn't stop it and the joy  on his face when he lifts that gas mask  from jamie's head the rapturous  joy at realizing that it's worked is  palpable and we are not done yet  jack returns to stop a bomb from  dropping on the site the doctor  sets up to spread the nana genes  once the disease now the cure  this is actually pretty big because  there really was  no giveaway prior to this that the  condition  was going to be reversible contrast that  was something like  the idiot's lantern where rose suffers a  fate that has already been met by  numerous people and we automatically  know that it's going to be reversed  because it's happened to a companion of  course they're going to undo it  but that's not the case here it is  completely possible that  all the people who've been changed won't  return to normal we can  trust that they'll be stopped and the  threat will be resolved but we can't be  sure that they'll actually be  saved but they are  this small army of gas masks zombies  are restored to once they once were and  we  get everybody lives  just this once everybody lives  that's not just a good line if you  consider two parters to be a single  story  then this is the eighth story of series  one and every  other one prior to this has seen people  die from clive to jabe gwyneth  multiple members of unit dozens of  security guards pete  and more and there is yet to be a story  without a casualty  and while we won't learn full details  for some time  we do know already that the doctor is  starting this series coming off of the  time war where he believes  himself to be the only survivor of his  home planet and his species  he's been seeing death every day and we  don't even  know for how long but finally  after all that he gets a day  where everybody lives and this sentiment  extends  way beyond the victims the bad guys turn  out to be a medical device that didn't  know it was making a mistake  there's no monster no actual villain  which means that  everybody lives means truly everybody  not every human but the invading aliens  are dead  there's no body count not even a fallen  foe  everybody absolutely everybody  lives and that's what the episode has  been building to  so what the episode before it was  building to it is what the entire series  up to this point has been building to  giving the doctor one truly glorious day  where nobody's sacrificed  nobody's abandoned nobody falls and  everybody lives this scene  is great in isolation but the weight of  what the show has given us up to that  point  along with what is clearly a deeply  painful background for the doctor  oh that puts it over the top without  that is the foundation  the scene wouldn't hit the way it does  i've loved this from the very first time  i saw it  but it has actually become more  meaningful to me  since i had the privilege of getting to  see and meet  christopher eccleston at gallifrey one  earlier this year before  everything got locked down one of the  things he was open about  was that his background as an actor  prior to doctor who was very  heavy serious dour material  and he was frequently uneasy when he was  called him on to be  happy or funny or joyous  that was kind of amazing to me because  his joyful moments were what hooked me  into his version of the doctor right  from the first episode  and there was no moment more joyful than  this  and i am delighted to say that i had the  chance to tell him that  when he signed the photo that i had  taken with him  we all have things  that we are uncomfortable doing or  showing  the world and for me at least there are  a few things more  reassuring than being told that the  thing that i worried about sharing  helped people connect with me  this scene gets absolutely everything  right the emotion the uplifting music a  perfectly pitched performance from  eccleston it all  comes together to make one fantastic  scene  thank you for watching i have a patreon  that supports me  and this channel please leave your  comments below share this video around  and  be sure to check out other videos in  this collaborative project  there are a ton of great creators in on  this  and maybe you'll even discover some new  ones they have picked  some really stellar scenes to cover and  i want you to please  remember through all this be well  be safe and be kind  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  now  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]

